  "source_guidance": [
   {
    "title": "Brief-COPE Total Score and Subscales",
    "text": "Scores are presented as average scores (sum of item scores divided by number of items) for each of the three overarching coping styles, indicating the degree to which the respondent has been engaging in that coping style.\n\nThe three subscales comprise the following items:\nProblem-Focused Coping (Items 2, 7, 10, 12, 14, 17, 23, 25): Active coping, use of informational support, planning, and positive reframing. Higher scores are generally considered better, and lower scores worse. Higher scores generally indicate psychological strength, grit, a practical approach to problem solving and is predictive of positive outcomes.\nEmotion-Focused Coping (Items 5, 9, 13, 15, 18, 20, 21, 22, 24, 26, 27, 28): Venting, emotional support, humour, acceptance, self-blame, and religion. High or low scores are not uniformly associated with psychological health or ill health, but can be used to inform a wider formulation of the respondent’s coping styles.\nAvoidant Coping (Items 1, 3, 4, 6, 8, 11, 16, 19): Self-distraction, denial, substance use, and behavioural disengagement. Higher scores indicate physical or cognitive efforts to disengage from the stressor. Low scores are typically indicative of adaptive coping.\n",
    "source_url": null
   },
   {
    "title": "Brief-COPE Percentiles",
    "text": "Two percentile comparisons are provided for each subscale. A normative percentile is presented based on data from a non-clinical sample of athletes (Poulus et al., 2020), helping to contextualise results in comparison to typical responses from non-clinical individuals. Additionally, a clinical percentile is presented which compares responses to clients receiving outpatient mental health services (Hegarty & Buchanan, 2021). A percentile of 50 represents an average score, whereas a percentile of 90 indicates the respondent scored higher than 90 percent of individuals in the reference group.\n",
    "source_url": null
   },
   {
    "title": "Brief-COPE Interpretation",
    "text": "During interpretation, it can be helpful to examine the pattern of responding across the three subscales. Consistently low scores on all subscales may indicate: (1) the respondent does not feel they have many stressors to cope with; (2) a lack of reflective capacity or resistance to disclose personal information; or (3) the respondent does not have many active coping skills.\nIndividual examination of the 14 facets and their constituent items can pinpoint specific adaptive or maladaptive coping strategies and be useful for eliciting discussion with the respondent. Facet-level scores are also presented to allow for more granular insights.\n",
    "source_url": null
   },
   {
    "title": "Normative Data for the Brief-COPE",
    "text": "Normative data have been established from multiple samples. Poulus and colleagues (2020) validated the scale among 316 esports athletes and found the following means and standard deviations for each subscale: Problem-Focused (M = 2.47, SD = 0.63), Emotion-Focused (M = 2.23, SD = 0.49), and Avoidant Coping (M = 1.64, SD = 0.45). This non-clinical sample data is used to compute normative percentile ranks. Additionally, NovoPsych compiled responses from patients receiving psychological intervention in outpatient settings (N = 3,635) to produce clinical normative data ( Hegarty & Buchanan, 2021 ). Both normative samples are used to provide context for interpreting individual scores.\nProfessional Access\n",
    "source_url": null
   }
  ],
  "source_tables": [],
  "unparsed_pdf_expressions": [
   {
